WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.400
Code was valuable because it was scarce.

2
00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.000
Today, here in DB1's auditorium,

3
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.950
we have a talk on AI from our client EBANX.

4
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.300
The topic of the talk is

5
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.300
what becomes of consultancies in this world of AI.

6
00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.200
We were in a semi-industrial era of software,

7
00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:22.400
where our main work was still handcrafted.

8
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.850
What changes in our industry in the agentic era?

9
00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.050
I'm Marlon, and I currently work at EBANX.

10
00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:35.600
For two years I have been researching, in real depth, the use of AI

11
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.450
in the context of software engineering.

12
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.750
I found a lot of theoretical reading on what an LLM is,

13
00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.900
on what RAG is.

14
00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.750
What I was missing, when I started researching,

15
00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.550
was something a little more applied.

16
00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.150
I mean, fine, great, I know what all of this is:

17
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.100
how do I take this pile of concepts and bring it into my day-to-day?

18
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.200
It is not just cost, is it?

19
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.550
Cost matters, obviously. Deadlines matter, obviously,

20
00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.600
since effort is something we have basically removed from the equation.

21
00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:03.400
The question is: fine, and now what?

22
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.950
Now that we have all this, how do we get a coherent result

23
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.050
and work out what to do from here?

24
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.750
How do we get a grip on the situation?

25
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.900
A lot changes, and a lot is changing.

26
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.200
How do you deal with all of that?

